<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/advanced/literary-theory">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Literary theory is <strong>the study of the lenses through which texts are read</strong> —
    formalist, structuralist, psychoanalytic, Marxist, feminist, postcolonial, reader-centred.
    Theory got a reputation for difficulty, but its core move is simple and permanent: every
    reading uses a lens, and knowing your lens is knowing what you're finding.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the major lenses, from text-alone to reader and politics" widget="equationStep" config={theoryCfg} />

  <h2>The theory wars, settled practically</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The theory/"just reading" dispute:</strong> there is no lens-free reading — "just the text" is formalism, "the author meant" is intentionalism. Theory's real achievement was making the lenses visible.</li>
    <li><strong>The canon argument:</strong> whose books get taught? The 1980s–90s canon wars opened syllabi to women, global and minority writers — the argument reshaped what literature IS, not just which books are assigned.</li>
    <li><strong>Postcolonial reading in practice:</strong> reading Heart of Darkness alongside Achebe's "An Image of Africa" (1975, calling Conrad "a thoroughgoing racist") — the same text under two lenses, both worth teaching.</li>
    <li><strong>The current synthesis:</strong> most working critics are pragmatists — close reading with historically informed, politically aware lenses, worn one at a time and named.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Culler, Literary Theory: A Very Short Introduction; Eagleton, Literary Theory: An Introduction (1983); Achebe (1975); Said (1978).</p>
</TopicPage>
